Driving Simulator for Assessing Driving Skills of People with Multiple Sclerosis: a Pilot Study
Camilla Pierella*, Paolo Romani*, Filippo Gandolfi, Jessica Podda, Antonino Massone, Andrea Tacchino, Giampaolo Brichetto, Andrea Canessa+, Serena Ricci+, and Maura Casadio+

Abstract— Driving is a common activity with a significant impact on the quality of life since enables independence and fosters social activities. Nevertheless, driving is a rather complex task that requires different skills, such as physical abilities, proper judgement, risks perception / evaluation. Both these physical and cognitive abilities can be impaired after Multiple Sclerosis (MS) and can additionally be affected by the individual emotional state and disease progression. Driving simulators can be a good solution to assess and train driving skills of people with MS, but there are currently few studies focused on MS and driving ability. Moreover, they mostly aim at understanding driving performance impairments, mostly during monotonous highway drives. Here, we describe the framework of a new personalized driving simulator ADRIS 2.0, and the results of a first test on a group of unimpaired young adults and of a pilot test with three MS and sex and age-matched control subjects. Subjects trained with ADRIS 2.0 for a session of 30 minutes, driving through scenarios with different levels of difficulties. The system resulted easy to use and able to provide a realistic driving experience for all three groups of subjects. It also provided quantitative metrics that described MS drivers slower and prone to commit more infractions and collisions than control subjects. Thus, the developed simulator resulted well tolerated by all users, capable of detecting differences between control and MS subjects and its potential could be further exploited into the design of a personalized training of MS people.
I. INTRODUCTION
Driving is a common daily life activity fostering independence and contributing significantly to the quality of our life: most of us drive to work, to medical appointments, to shop and for leisure. However, driving is a complex activity, involving different sensory, motor, and cognitive abilities, that can be affected by MS [1]. There is evidence that 20% to 40% of drivers with MS fail fitness-to-drive assessment [1], [2] and have a greater chance of getting in a car accident than other drivers [3]. This is due to impairments in cognition, attention, visual information perception and processing, executive function, visuospatial ability, and visual acuity that interrupt driver information processing at several levels (e.g., perception, attention to a stimulus, interpretation of the

situation on the road, formulation of a plan, execution of an action, corrective actions) [4]. In the relapsing remitting cases of MS there is the challenge of the unpredictability of their symptoms, that can appear suddenly, impairing the ability to drive, then disappearing and allowing people to drive again, until the following episode. Conversely, people with progressive forms of MS may experience a slow worsening of abilities that increasingly affects driving. Last but not least, the medications prescribed for some symptoms, can lead to drowsiness or decreased coordination and attention [5]. In summary, the complexity and the multi-factorial aspects of MS make each case unique and to be considered individually, with highly personalized assessment and training tools. Despite the importance of this problem and today’s technological advances, it is still difficult to find reliable and available solutions for driving skills assessment and training.
A possible way to practice and assess driving is by using simulators [6]. In fact, VR-based systems represent an attractive option to consistently study re-learning processes by replicating situations that users usually experience while driving in the real world, albeit in a riskless environment [7]. With VR, one can create controlled, yet complex and dynamic environments and extend the capabilities that the real-world limits. In the context of neurorehabilitation, VR can be used to promote cognitive and motor functional recovery [8], [9], enhancing motor learning and retention, with tasks requiring planning, attention, information processing of stimuli in the virtual environment and sensory integration. Importantly, the performance obtained while using driving simulators could be a good predictor for patterns of actual driving, both in terms of successful driving, as well as indication of possible deficits [10], [11]. However, currently available driving simulators are not specifically designed or customized to assess and train people with MS. Another important main limitation of the currently available solutions is the lack of possibility to create and personalize scenarios with different levels of difficulty [12], [13]. Indeed, they mainly follow one-fits-all approach, or have limited personalization features, thus making them not suitable for addressing the very different needs of people with such a multifaceted pathology like MS. These findings suggest

