This work has a technological and a scientific objective. The technological objective consisted in the development of an updated version of the Accesible DRIving Simulator ADRIS [14], [15] , making it highly personalized and easy to use by both people with MS and their therapists. The scientific aim consisted in pilot testing the new simulator and its usability for people with MS. To do so, we tested ADRIS 2.0 first with a group of 7 unimpaired young adults; then with a small cohort of MS subjects and age- and sex- matched control subjects, and identified an initial set of indicators capable to assess driving performance for MS in different environments.
II. MATERIAL AND METHODS
A. ADRIS 2.0
The simulator is composed of three main parts: a PC, a driving controller (i.e., gaming steering wheel and pedals) and

a computer screen. The system could be also used with an immersive virtual reality (VR) using a head mounted display, as well as it could be controlled with a joystick, allowing people with motor disability to drive.
The software architecture is based on Unreal Engine. It includes a graphic interface, see Fig.1, easy to use by different users (i.e., MS people for training purposes, therapists for evaluations), an artificial intelligence (AI) managing the simulations, realistic sounds, and it supports the creation of customized scenarios. Within the simulator, it is possible to control several variables such as: traffic volume, weather and light conditions. Furthermore, it is possible to generate unexpected events either static (e.g. obstacles in the street, a broke down vehicle) or dynamic (e.g. pedestrians or riders suddenly crossing the street; vehicles running stops or red lights). Therefore, modifying light (from daylight to dark), weather (sunny, raining or foggy), and unexpected events, nine scenarios (Fig.1, bottom) with different environments and level of difficulty have been developed. An example of the urban environment, with traffic during daylight and the scenario in a highway at sunset are shown in Fig.2. At the end of the simulation (which can be stopped when the driver reaches a specific location, or a predetermined driving distance

