C. Experimental Set-Up and Protocol
In both tests, the one with young subjects and the pilot test with MS subjects and relative matched controls, subjects were required to use the single monitor version of the simulator ADRIS 2.0 as if they were driving a real car. Participants recruited in test 1 drove for about one hour while participants recruited in experiment 2 drove for 30 minutes to avoid fatigue for people with MS. Participants, using the pedals and/or steering wheels as controllers (all control subjects and two MS subjects used both, while one MS subject used steering wheel controls), freely drove for around 5 minutes on an open map to familiarize with the simulator (free drive, FD). Then, they performed a first session where they were asked to follow a leading vehicle, followed by nine sessions of different difficulties in terms of road (urban, motorway, rural), weather, light condition, and traffic volume, see Fig. 3. Each session/scenario was characterized by a main event, and the user had to follow the directions of the experimenter. In test 2 the experimental protocol was shorter as subjects drove through 3 out of the 9 scenarios. Specifically, the first scenario (characterized by low traffic, good weather and lights conditions) began with the driver at a road roundabout. During the driving, after a turn there was a garbage bin in the middle of the street. The user must avoid it, while other background vehicles could collide on it. The second session Scenario 3 , which started in an expressway, had medium traffic condition, good weather and sunset lights. While taking the first exit, a pedestrian passed through the street and the driver had to brake to avoid him. The third level Scenario 7 -, with high traffic and night lights conditions, tested the user on the ability to drive in a rural scenario and is characterized by a landslide suddenly falling. The experiment ended with another leading-vehicle session, equal to the initial one.

