At the end of the experiment, all participants filled out three questionnaires: Simulation Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ)[16]; NASA Task Load Index (NASA TLX)[17] and Igroup presence questionnaire (IPQ)[18].
D. Data Analysis
To analyze the performance, we extracted the following parameters: number of infractions (i.e., lane crossing, running red-light and running stop), number of collisions (e.g., with the leading car, static or dynamic obstacle), maximum speed, mean speed, and variability of the mean speed, considering only when the car was moving (i.e., speed > 3 Km/h). Speed variability indicates whether the driver maintained the same speed during the driving and is an indicator of how much the driver is at ease during the driving.
III. RESULTS
Test 1. In the SSQ all young adults participants rated general discomfort (median: 2 range 1-9) and motion sickness (nausea median: 2 range 1-8; stomach awareness median: 1 range: 1-7; vertigo median: 3 range: 1-8) as low, with exception of 2 subjects who reported higher levels of sickness. These 2 subjects also reported a higher level of eyestrain (median: 3 range: 2-8) and blurred vision (median: 2 range: 18). However, most of the participants did not report any vision related discomfort. In the NASA TLX survey they reported a low physical fatigue (median 2 range:1-4) and a moderate level of mental effort (median: 4 range 2-7). When we ask the participants which level of performance they reached, responses ranges from 2 to 9 (median: 5) suggesting that the self-assessment of driving performance was extremely subjective. Results of the IPQ questionnaire, showed that most of the participants defined the simulation coherent with the real experience (median: 6 range: 4-7) and perceived the virtual world with good fidelity with respect to the reality (median: 3 range: 3-7). They also reported a good immersion inside the virtual world (feeling of being surrounded median: 6 range: 47; sense of presence in virtual space median: 6 range: 5-7). 3 out of 7 subjects were highly captured by the virtual world while the rest reported a moderate feeling of fascination (median: 6 range: 3-9).
As for the driving performance, the data collected with the simulator highlighted a low number of collisions and infractions (Collisions median: 1 range:0-4 ; Infractions median: 2 range: 1-5), see Fig. 4. For what concerns the speed, data show a low variability on the maximum and mean speed (Max Speed median: 93 Km/h range: 77-107 Km/h, Mean Speed median: 31 Km/h range: 29-32 Km/h). In particular, the low Mean Speed values could be explained by the high density of traffic lights and the increase of traffic density and adverse conditions through the levels.
Test 2. Results of the second group of subjects, MS subjects and their matched control subjects, were also encouraging. From the SSQ questionnaire subjects reported no general discomfort (median: 1 range: 1-3), no physical fatigue (median: 1, range: 1-5). However, 2 out of 3 controls reported high levels of motion sickness symptoms mostly related to nausea. The NASA TLX survey reported moderate physical

fatigue (median: 3 range: 1-9) and a moderate level of mental effort (median: 3,5 range: 2-8) and a low/moderate level of frustration (median: 3 range: 1-5). Finally, IPQ showed that participants were fascinated by the virtual experience (median: 8 range: 5-9), thus suggesting that also people with MS would be motivated and engaged in using the simulator.
